  "actual_usd": 0.0004071256,
  "answer": "The public help article should clarify that precise page and paragraph-level citations are fully supported **only for natively digital, text-searchable PDFs**, not for scanned, photocopied, or image-only PDFs [NL-002#NL-002-S09]. For scanned PDFs, the system relies on OCR, which is a heuristic process; if the scan is blurry, low-resolution, contains handwriting, or uses complex layouts, the OCR output may contain broken paragraphs or misread characters, directly causing broken citations or a failure to link to the exact paragraph [NL-006#NL-006-S03]. In practice, this means citations for scanned PDFs can be missing, point to page one of a multi-page document, or fail to generate a citation anchor at all [NL-011#NL-011-S05], [NL-018#NL-018-S04]. The system may also state it cannot find the answer even when the information exists on the scanned page [NL-028#NL-028-S03], and in some cases the citation is stripped entirely due to a format limitation [NL-006#NL-006-S04].\n\nANSWER_STATUS: answer\nUSED_MEMORY_CLAIMS: none",
